# The First Crossing of the Whispering Bridge

When the Whispering Bridge first appeared between the two villages, none knew whether the ancient promise was true—would offerings truly triple as they crossed?

The first brave soul, a farmer, stood at the bridge's edge with five sacks of grain. Her hands trembled. Should she send all and risk everything? Should she send nothing and stay safe but poor?

She chose the middle path. Nearly half her grain—two sacks—she rolled onto the bridge. The wood hummed its strange song as the sacks crossed and became six.

On the far side, a stranger caught them. He too faced a choice. Would he keep all? Return nothing? 

The stranger understood: this farmer had risked scarcity to create abundance for both. He returned three sacks—not everything, but enough to show he valued her courage. Both villages ate better that night than they had in years.

The Bridge keepers smiled. "Trust begins not with certainty," they whispered, "but with the willingness to reach halfway across the darkness, hoping another hand reaches back."

The farmer and stranger never met, but their first tentative exchange wrote the opening verse of a longer story—one that would test them many times more.
